Z||000000||Seanh||03-15-2001||06:36 PM||||To put it plain and simple, I cant do it at all. First off, I can't see how anyone could look at the timeing mark (this is with engine sripped down, much less doing timeing on a normal engine). I can never get it right, because I cant see the timeing mark. Is there any other way to do it, that doesnt involve any thing to do with that damn mark??? Is there another configuration of up and down lifters that I can look for that will tell me when piston no.1 is at TDC? Anyone have an easier way of doing this? Dont think about looking for marks on the studs, the heads were redone and cleaned.||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||03-15-2001||06:46 PM||||You say the engine is stripped down.  Is it on a stand, then, out of the car?  If the timing cover is off, you can look at the marks on the timing gears.<P>I bet this doesn't help, but I don't think I really understand your situation.  And, for what it's worth, the crank position isn't ultra-critical for lashing hydraulic valve lifters.  You just need to be sure the lifter is "down," on the root diameter of the cam, when you lash it - which is most of the way around the cam, anyway.      <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 03-15-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000002||hugh||03-15-2001||06:51 PM||||Take the spark plugs out so you can turn the engine over easier by hand.turn the engine over slowly in the direction it normally turns while you try to hold back the compression of number 1 cylinder.You will be able to tell when you have gone too far.Then just back it up a little and you are very close to tdc.Adjust your valves.Then go to the next cylinder in the firing order,which will be close to where you are now,and repeat the compression check then adjust those valves.Repeat till all valves are done.It will take some time,but you'll be surprised how easy it is.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||03-15-2001||06:53 PM||||No, the engine is now in the car, finally. I only have it torn down to the heads. All intake manifolds crap is all off.<P>Actually, StuGood, you just helped me out alot. I was wondering if I could just set one valve lash when the other valve(same cyliner) is up. That pretty much answered my question||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||03-15-2001||06:57 PM||||I am also curious, (never done valve lash before) why somtimes the little "thing" inside the lifter, where the pushrod sits, goes down into the lifter, when the outside part of the lifter stays up? I know those are not the best words to describe the situation, but I am still learing about this as I go.<P>Hugh: I can't belive I didn't think of that. I am pretty stupid at this though. That solution is so simple, its not often thought of. Thanks<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 03-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||03-15-2001||08:49 PM||||The reason the plunger goes down into the lifter is to assist in lubrication to the top of heads and reduce valve noise. This is why when you set the lash you want half travel into the lifter. Don't bottom them out, you will bend push rods!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||03-15-2001||11:46 PM||||Great. I did it, I think. I did the valve lash, and installed the lower manifold. I will be doing the rest tomarrow. Thanks for the fast help!||||reg||1
